# Mantine components and theme

The web app uses `@mantine/core` with the forest theme in `apps/web/app/theme.ts`. Fetch Mantine LLM docs before writing Mantine code: `https://mantine.dev/llms.txt`, then the matching page under `https://mantine.dev/llms/` (for example `core-button.md`). Do not invent props, styles APIs, or package names.

## Prefer Mantine

Before adding a custom atom or molecule, check `@mantine/core`. Use Button, Title, Text, Anchor, Stack, Group, Card, Alert, Loader, TextInput, and similar when they exist.

Import Mantine directly, or keep a thin wrapper that only narrows props (like `atoms/Button`). Do not rebuild those controls in CSS Modules.

The app template `AppShell` is the page skeleton. Mantine `AppShell` is a different layout primitive. Use it only when the header/navbar/aside API is needed.

## Theme tokens

Colors, spacing, and radius come from `theme.ts` and `--mantine-*` CSS variables. Semantic aliases in `styles/tokens.css` (`--color-text`, `--space-md`) wrap those variables. No raw hex, rgb, or oklch in components.

```tsx
// Good
import { Button, Title } from '@mantine/core';

export function EmptyGardens({ onRetry }: { onRetry: () => void }) {
  return (
    <>
      <Title order={1}>Gardens</Title>
      <Button onClick={onRetry}>Retry</Button>
    </>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// Bad — skip Mantine, hardcode color
import styles from './Card.module.css';

export function Card({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <div className={`card ${styles.root}`} style={{ color: '#228B22' }}>
      {children}
    </div>
  );
}
```
